     I think we've been going in circles here, but with
luck it's actually started at the outer end of a spiral
and we're narrowing to the center.

     If you're using any of the Makefiles in the sys\winnt
directory, then you are using the DLB configuration after
all.  Even though config.h has ``#define DLB'' commented
out, those Makefiles all use a compiler command line
option to predefine DLB, so by the time config.h is used,
DLB has already been enabled and uncommenting that line
wouldn't change anything.  (There's an option in all
three of the winnt Makefiles to override this, but it
isn't mentioned in Install.nt.)

     You haven't specified the actual commands you've
been using to perform a successful full build+install.
I'm going to assume that you're using nmake according to
Install.nt's directions for Microsoft's compiler.  You'll
need to make two small changes to sys\winnt\Makefile.msc
(and after it's been modified, re-run nhsetup.bat to put
the new copy into the right place).  Add $(DAT)\foo2.des
to the list of files after $(O)sp_lev.tag (it has a long
space separated list, using trailing backslash to
continue across multiple lines; the new entry could go
just about anywhere but might as well be placed after
wizard.des).  And a few lines below that, you need to
add the command to build foo2.lev from foo2.des (copy
the ``$(U)lev_comp wizard.des'' line and then change
wizard to foo2 in the copy; the lev_comp commands are
intended as separate lines so don't include any trailing
backslashes).

     Those two changes will tell nmake when and how to
build foo2.lev.  The subsequent commands which build
nhdat operate on *.lev so won't need to be modified to
explicitly add the new one.  (There is actually a third
change to make, in the list of commands below "spotless:"
you should include a new line to delete foo2.lev so that
``nmake spotless'' will get rid of it along with all the
other constructed files.)

     After that, ``nmake install'' will include your
level in the nhdat file placed into the "binary" (aka
playground) directory it sets up.  It is feasible to
take some shortcuts after modifying things and avoid
a full install, but is probably safer not to.  Most
constructed files that aren't affected by future
changes won't need to be rebuilt, so "full install"
should go much faster than it does on the first time.
(If you need or want to revert to unmodified nethack
you'll need to first ``nmake spotless'' or manually
delete foo2.lev so that any eventual *.lev processing
won't include it, then revert to the original
Makefile.* in sys\winnt, and also re-run nhsetup.bat
again so that the reverted Makefile gets copied to the
right place.)


